<%# A step whose instruction is more than one move shows one frame per move, hung off a rail with
    a numbered node apiece: the same rail and node the step timeline itself uses, so the order
    reads left to right without an arrow wedged into every gap. The number is the move, the tab is
    the floor it is taken on, and the caption is the one thing that frame proves.

    The rail is drawn a segment at a time, from each node to the next, rather than as one bar
    across the whole strip. That is what lets it turn: stacked, the moves are as tall as their
    captions make them, and a single bar would have to know the height of the last one to stop at
    its node rather than run on past it.

    The frame width comes off the count (--n2 draws each capture at 3x, --n3 at 2x) so the pixels
    always land on whole blocks. %>
<div class="pn-wt-strip-fit">
  <div class="pn-wt-strip pn-wt-strip--n<%= shots.size %>">
    <% shots.each_with_index do |shot, i| %>
      <div class="pn-wt-strip__move">
        <div class="pn-wt-strip__node" aria-hidden="true"><%= i + 1 %></div>
        <% unless i == shots.size - 1 %>
          <div class="pn-wt-strip__link" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <% end %>
        <%= render "shot", shot: shot %>
        <% if shot.caption? %>
          <div class="pn-wt-strip__caption"><%= shot_caption(step, shot) %></div>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
